Money, finance, and macroeconomic systems (@money-finance-and-macroeconomic-systems: 0, is #chapter, #outline) { r_5XNVJW39BEKCCDC97ABTDMD98H
This material examines money, banking, credit, finance, employment, output, inflation, growth, and instability as interacting economic and institutional processes. Connect household and organisational activity with monetary systems, public finance, investment, international flows, and the capacity to maintain infrastructure. Routes introduce quantities, accounting relationships, models, and historical evidence at the depth needed for their question. Compare competing explanations and make their assumptions explicit when interpreting an economy or a proposed intervention.

**Parent:** [Political-Economy](../Political-Economy.lingua#r_2WPG1Y767BM24Z0K4BY6Y6T62W).
